<section xml:id="isosceles-triangle-angles">
  <title>3.1.7 Playfair's Axiom Implies the Alternate Interior Angle Converse Theorem</title>

  <proof>
    <p>
      Consider a line <me>l</me> and a point <me>A</me> not on that line.
    </p>

    <p>
      Playfair's axiom states that there is exactly one line through
      <me>A</me> parallel to <me>l</me>. Now draw a transversal through
      <me>A</me> that intersects <me>l</me>. Let <me>\alpha</me> and
      <me>\beta</me> denote a pair of alternate interior angles formed
      by the transversal and the two lines. Suppose that
      <me>m\angle\alpha \ne m\angle\beta</me>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can construct a line through <me>A</me> such that its alternate
      interior angle is congruent to <me>\beta</me>. Denote this new
      angle by <me>\alpha'</me>, so that
      <me>m\angle\alpha' = m\angle\beta</me>.
    </p>

    <p>
      By the Alternate Interior Angle Theorem, this new line is parallel
      to <me>l</me>. Thus, we have constructed a line through <me>A</me>
      that is parallel to <me>l</me> and distinct from the original
      parallel line.
    </p>

    <p>
      This contradicts Playfair's axiom, which states that there is
      exactly one line through <me>A</me> parallel to <me>l</me>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore,
      <me>m\angle\alpha = m\angle\beta</me>,
      and hence Playfair's axiom implies the Alternate Interior Angle
      Converse Theorem.
    </p>
  </proof>
</section>